    "narrative": "The Austin Waves offensive engine is led today by **Jackson Merrill**, whose 74.59 score represents the ceiling play for this slate. Facing G. Holmes (xERA 4.47), **Jackson Merrill** benefits from a massive 13% dynamic platoon boost and a prime #2 slot in the order, positioning him to maximize both run production and RBI. Joining him at the top of our priority list is **Kyle Tucker** (66.62) and **Gunnar Henderson** (67.48). While **Gunnar Henderson**\u2019s score is technically contingent on a pending lineup confirmation at #2, his matchup against the soft-tossing Aldegheri (5.75 xERA) provides an elite 19% SP skill advantage. We are also monitoring **Andy Pages** (62.80); while his raw metrics are strong, the inclusion of a \"Slow SP Delivery\" variable\u2014which statistically boosts his stolen base potential against the opposing battery\u2014makes him a high-efficiency anchor in the outfield.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved the marginal utility spots, ultimately resulting in **Miguel Vargas** and **Nolan Schanuel** landing on the bench. **Miguel Vargas** (47.86) was the most difficult cut; despite his favorable #2 spot in the order, his efficiency was suppressed by a matchup against Williams, whose profile mitigates the stolen base threat\u2014a non-negotiable metric for our roster construction. Similarly, **Wade Meckler** (40.42) remains in the lineup over the bench depth solely due to his positional necessity and platoon advantage against Bradish, despite his score teetering near our 40.0 floor. \n\nRisks today are concentrated on our \"Lineup Pending\" designations, specifically **Gunnar Henderson** and **Coby Mayo**. While both are projected for heavy run-scoring opportunities against Aldegheri, any shuffling in the batting order\u2014specifically moving **Coby Mayo** out of the #5 spot\u2014could compress their efficiency scores toward our 40-point threshold. We are comfortable with **Drake Baldwin** (59.70) as our primary catcher, as his xwOBA of .380 against King suggests he is under-projected by raw models, provided he maintains his projected #2 spot in the batting order.",
